<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.10.1: Graphs of Linear Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01903</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.10.1: Graphs of Linear Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>97594100-8099-4879-912b-227b1f0d5c94</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Here is a graph of the equation \(y=8-2x\).</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_02_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Where do you see the 8 from the equation in the graph?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">The 8 in \(y=8-2x\) means the graph intersects the \(y\)-axis at 8. The \(y\)-intercept is (0,8).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Where do you see the –2 from the equation in the graph?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">The –2 is the slope of the line. You can see it, for example, by looking at \((0,8)\) and \((1,6)\). When \(x\) increases by 1, \(y\) decreases by 2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_2ce36a44-7afd-4ec4-b992-74d1eaaf0c8c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            
            <para id="para-00009">What is the value of \(x\) for the \(x\)-intercept of the graph? How does this relate to the equation?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00010">Correct! The \(x\)-intercept is \((4,0)\). The 4 is the solution of \(8-2x=0\).</para>
            <para id="para-00011">The correct answer is 4. The \(x\)-intercept is \((4,0)\). The 4 is the solution of \(8-2x=0\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content></document>